    "input": "search_document: I understand your frustration and that you are seeking an immediate resolution for a cracked frame on a new mountain bike. Based strictly on the provided handbook excerpts, here is the information regarding your specific situation:\n\n**1. Warranty Coverage for Defects**\nThe standard 2-year warranty (730 days) explicitly covers \"defects in materials and workmanship under normal use.\" If a defect is found within this window, Northwind Outdoor will repair or replace the item at no cost to you [warranty]. A cracked frame would typically fall under a defect in materials or workmanship, provided it is not the result of misuse.\n\n**2. Extended Warranty Misconception**\nThe extended warranty option (5 years for 15% of the price) is an *upgrade* to the standard coverage period. It does not change what is covered; it only extends the time frame. The standard warranty already covers manufacturing defects for the first 2 years [warranty]. Therefore, the extended warranty is not required to claim a defect that occurred immediately after purchase, as long as it is within the standard 730-day window [warranty].\n\n**3. Damaged Item vs. Warranty Defect**\nIf the crack was present upon delivery or occurred due to shipping, this may be treated as a \"Damaged Item\" rather than a standard warranty claim.\n*   **Reporting Deadline:** You must report visible damage within **48 hours** of delivery [damaged_items].\n*   **Evidence:** You must upload at least 3 high-resolution photos (defect, overall item, packaging) [damaged_items].\n*   **Resolution for Major Structural Failure:** A cracked frame is considered a \"major structural failure.\" For such failures, Northwind Outdoor issues a **full refund** of the item\u2019s price, up to a maximum of **$500.00 per item** [damaged_items].\n*   **High-Value Items:** Since you mentioned a \"high-end\" bike, if the price exceeds $500.00, the claim requires a **manual review by the senior support team**, which may take up to 5 business days [damaged_items].\n\n**4. Misuse Exclusions**\nIf the crack is determined to be caused by improper use (e.g., overloading or impact), the claim will be denied, and you would be responsible for return shipping costs [damaged_items]. However, if it is a manufacturing defect, it is covered under the standard warranty [warranty].\n\n**5. Request for Human Agent**\nThe handbook excerpts do not contain a specific protocol for bypassing automated support or forcing an immediate human handoff. However, since your item likely exceeds the $500.00 automatic refund cap for damaged items and requires a manual review by the senior support team [damaged_items], this process inherently involves human review.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n1.  If you are within 48 hours of delivery, immediately log into your account and select \"Report Damage,\" uploading the required 3 photos [damaged_items].\n2.  If you are outside the 48-hour window but within the 730-day warranty period, file a standard warranty claim for a defect in materials/workmanship [warranty].\n3.  Because the item is high-value and potentially subject to manual review, I will escalate this ticket to a human agent to assist with the specific claim processing and review, as the handbook indicates senior support involvement is required for items over $500.00 [damaged_items].\n\n*Note: The handbook does not specify a \"full replacement\" as the automatic outcome for damaged items over $500; it specifies a full refund up to $500 and manual review for higher values. For warranty defects, it specifies repair or replacement [warranty].*",
